<<<
one spelling only, & extra- is recommended.
===
one spelling only, & *extra-* is recommended.
>>>

<<<
extricate makes -cable; see -ABLE 1.
===
**extricate** makes *-cable*; see ^^-ABLE^^ 1.
>>>

<<<
extrinsic. See EXTERIOR.
===
**extrinsic.** See ^^EXTERIOR^^.
>>>

<<<
-EY & -Y IN ADJECTIVES. The adjectival suffix is -y, not -ey.
===
**-EY & -Y IN ADJECTIVES.** The adjectival suffix is *-y*, not *-ey*.
>>>

<<<
when -y is appended to nouns in Mure E (as mite), the e is to be
===
when *-y* is appended to nouns in ^^MUTE E^^ (as *mite*), the *e* is to be
>>>

<<<
dropped (mity, not miiey).
===
dropped (*mity*, not *mitey*).
>>>

<<<
A selection of the commonest -y adjectives from nouns in mute -e will suffice
===
A selection of the commonest *-y* adjectives from nouns in mute *-e* will suffice
>>>

<<<
if he drops the e—a needless fear. The safe -y adjectives are: bony, breezy, briny, crazy, easy, fleecy, fluky, gory, greasy, grimy, hasty, icy, lacy, mazy, miry, noisy, Oozy,
===
if he drops the *e*—a needless fear. The safe *-y* adjectives are: *bony, breezy, briny, crazy, easy, fleecy, fluky, gory, greasy, grimy, hasty, icy, lacy, mazy, miry, noisy, oozy,*
>>>

<<<
prosy, racy, rosy, scaly, shady, shaky, slimy, smoky, snaky, spicy, spiky, spongy, stony, wiry.
===
*prosy, racy, rosy, scaly, shady, shaky, slimy, smoky, snaky, spicy, spiky, spongy, stony, wiry.*
>>>

<<<
The shaky -y adjectives are: caky, cany, chancy, fluty, gamy, homy, horsy, liny, mity, mousy, nervy, nosy,
===
The shaky *-y* adjectives are: *caky, cany, chancy, fluty, gamy, homy, horsy, liny, mity, mousy, nervy, nosy,*
>>>

<<<
pursy, sidy, stagy, tuny, wavy, whity.
===
*pursy, sidy, stagy, tuny, wavy, whity.*
>>>

<<<
1. When an adjective in -y is made from a noun in -y, e is inserted to part y from -y: clayey & skyey, not clayy or skyy.
===
1. When an adjective in *-y* is made from a noun in *-y*, *e* is inserted to part *y* from *-y*: *clayey* & *skyey*, not *clayy* or *skyy*.
>>>

<<<
2. Hole makes holey, to prevent confusion with holy = hallowed.
===
2. *Hole* makes *holey*, to prevent confusion with *holy* = hallowed.
>>>

<<<
3. Adjectives from nouns in -we (00) retain the e: gluey & bluey, not gluy or bluy.
===
3. Adjectives from nouns in *-ue* (o͞o) retain the *e*: *gluey* & *bluey*, not *gluy* or *bluy*.
>>>

<<<
eye, vb, makes eying; see VERBS IN -IE &c¢., 7.
===
**eye,** vb, makes *eying*; see ^^VERBS IN -IE^^ &c., 7.
>>>

<<<
-EY, -IE, -Y, IN DIMINUTIVES. The most established type of all (baby, daddy, granny) has -y; most proper names (Tommy, Polly) have -y; it | would be a simplification if -y could be made universal: but -ie & -ey
===
**-EY, -IE, -Y, IN DIMINUTIVES.** The most established type of all (*baby, daddy, granny*) has *-y*; most proper names (*Tommy, Polly*) have *-y*; it would be a simplification if *-y* could be made universal: but *-ie* & *-ey*
>>>

<<<
are the only forms in some proper names (Charlie or Charley, never Charly; Minnie; Sukey); -ie is preferred in Scotland, the native
===
are the only forms in some proper names (*Charlie* or *Charley*, never *Charly*; *Minnie*; *Sukey*); *-ie* is preferred in Scotland, the native
>>>

<<<
land of some diminutives (laddie, lassie, caddie); the retention of mute -€, giving -ey (dovey, lovey, Nosey, &c.) is more defensible than in the
===
land of some diminutives (*laddie, lassie, caddie*); the retention of mute *-e*, giving *-ey* (*dovey, lovey, Nosey*, &c.) is more defensible than in the
>>>

<<<
adjectives made with -y (see -EY & -Y IN ADJECTIVES); & generally variety seems unavoidable.
===
adjectives made with *-y* (see ^^-EY & -Y IN ADJECTIVES^^); & generally variety seems unavoidable.
>>>

<<<
has been to recommend plain -y wherever
===
has been to recommend plain *-y* wherever
>>>

<<<
Some of the words included (booby, caddy, looby, Mary, Marie, puppy, rooty, toddy, & perhaps others)
===
Some of the words included (*booby, caddy, looby, Mary, Marie, puppy, rooty, toddy*, & perhaps others)
>>>

<<<
(frillies, goodies, Johnnies, kiddies, kilttes, sweeties) to think that -ie must be the singular termination, Adjectives
===
(*frillies, goodies, Johnnies, kiddies, kilties, sweeties*) to think that *-ie* must be the singular termination. Adjectives
>>>

<<<
like comfy are given here because the -y is the diminutive ending, & not the adjec- . tive suffix.
===
like *comfy* are given here because the *-y* is the diminutive ending, & not the adjective suffix.
>>>

<<<
Amy; Annie; Arty= Arthur (-ie); aunty (-ie); baby; Betty; Billy; billy = cooking-pot;
===
*Amy; Annie; Arty* = Arthur (*-ie*); *aunty* (*-ie*); *baby; Betty; Billy; billy* = cooking-pot;
>>>

<<<
blacky; Bobby; bobby = policeman; Bony = Bonaparte (-ey); booby; bookie = bookmaker;
===
*blacky; Bobby; bobby* = policeman; *Bony* = Bonaparte (*-ey*); *booby; bookie* = bookmaker;
>>>

<<<
bubby = brother or breast; bunny; caddie = golf-attendant; caddy = teabox;
===
*bubby* = brother or breast; *bunny*; *caddie* = golf-attendant; *caddy* = tea-box;
>>>

<<<
Carry (-ie); Charlie (-ey); Cissy = Cecilia (-ie); comfy; cooky = cook dear (-ie);
===
*Carry* (*-ie*); *Charlie* (*-ey*); *Cissy* = Cecilia (*-ie*); *comfy*; *cooky* = cook dear (*-ie*);
>>>

<<<
cookie=cake; coolie; corbie=crow; daddy; darky (-ey); deary (-ie); Dicky; doggy (-ie);
===
*cookie* = cake; *coolie*; *corbie* = crow; *daddy*; *darky* (*-ey*); *deary* (*-ie*); *Dicky*; *doggy* (*-ie*);
>>>

<<<
Dolly, d.; dovey; ducky; Hddy = Edward; Edy= Edith (-te); Effie; Emmy;
===
*Dolly,* d.; *dovey*; *ducky*; *Eddy* = Edward; *Edy* = Edith (*-ie*); *Effie*; *Emmy*;
>>>

<<<
Fanny; fatty; Florrie; Freddy; frilly; Froggy; Georgy = George;
===
*Fanny*; *fatty*; *Florrie*; *Freddy*; *frilly*; *Froggy*; *Georgy* = George;
>>>

<<<
Georgie = Georgina; girly (-ie); goody = goodwife or sweetmeat; goosy; granny; Hetty;
===
*Georgie* = Georgina; *girly* (*-ie*); *goody* = goodwife or sweetmeat; *goosy*; *granny*; *Hetty*;
>>>

<<<
hoodie = crow: hubby; Jacky; Jamy (-te)$
===
*hoodie* = crow: *hubby*; *Jacky*; *Jamy* (*-ie*);
>>>
